From 258e8e260b242df73a498f4b4f5ab1a15a19261a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 16:08:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 73/98] kernel/net: RFC 5961 RST, SCM_RIGHTS leaks, ioctl
 copyin, ARP merge (audit HIGH)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- tcp: an incoming RST matching the 4-tuple killed the connection with no
  sequence check (off-path reset/injection).  Add RFC 5961 §3.2: drop RSTs
  outside the receive window, reset only on an exact rcv_nxt match, send a
  challenge ACK otherwise.  Listener close left accept-queued ESTABLISHED
  and SYN_RECEIVED children unreaped (leak; peers think the link is live) —
  detach, kill to CLOSED, and RST the peers.
- af_unix: SCM_RIGHTS in-flight fds leaked on socket close (rx_fdq never
  drained) and on the recvmsg out-of-fds rollback (table slot cleared but
  the ref not dropped).  Release each via file_close_ptr; rollback now
  delivers nothing and re-queues.  sendmsg/recvmsg dereferenced the msghdr,
  iov, and cmsg straight from user pointers (recvmsg also wrote them back) —
  copyin the msghdr/iov/control into bounded kernel buffers, parse and build
  there, copyout the result.
- pty master + af_inet ioctls dereferenced the raw user `arg` (SIOCGIFCONF a
  kernel-write-through-user-pointer); route all through copyin/copyout.
- arp: arp_input inserted the sender of any packet (unsolicited-reply cache
  poisoning).  RFC 826 merge rule: always refresh an existing entry, but
  only insert when the packet is addressed to us (the solicited cases).

Found by a security audit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
